WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.633
File handling deserves special care because file names, paths, content type claims, archives, and metadata can all be influenced by users or external systems.

2
00:00:10.633 --> 00:00:15.044
A file upload feature should treat the uploaded file as untrusted.

3
00:00:15.044 --> 00:00:29.143
The application should control storage location, generated names, size limits, allowed types, scanning or review requirements, and access permissions rather than trusting client-provided names or headers.

4
00:00:29.143 --> 00:00:35.050
Path traversal occurs when input influences file access outside an intended directory.

5
00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:47.810
The defensive idea is to avoid using raw user input as a path, normalize and constrain paths carefully, use allowlists where possible, and separate public files from private internal storage.

6
00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:55.765
Temporary files should have predictable lifecycle management so sensitive data does not remain longer than intended.

7
00:00:55.765 --> 00:00:56.711
Node.

8
00:00:56.711 --> 00:01:01.909
js can launch child processes, but unsafe command construction is dangerous.

9
00:01:01.909 --> 00:01:06.714
User-controlled input should not be concatenated into shell commands.

10
00:01:06.714 --> 00:01:18.450
Prefer APIs that pass arguments without shell interpretation, restrict what operations are allowed, and apply operating system permissions, timeouts, and resource limits.

11
00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:25.460
The application should not rely on one validation check as the only barrier before a system-level effect.

12
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:34.045
Server-side request forgery, or SSRF, is a risk when server-side URL fetching can be influenced to access unintended resources.

13
00:01:34.045 --> 00:01:43.261
The service may be able to reach internal networks, cloud metadata endpoints, admin panels, or services that users cannot reach directly.

14
00:01:43.261 --> 00:01:54.760
Safe outbound request controls include allowlists, URL parsing discipline, network egress restrictions, timeouts, response limits, and careful handling of redirects.
